d used the  space  it just shows how much you know he knows  this case and how much he's invested in  it  and terry was it strategic for the  defense to hold off on presenting its  opening statement was that a good idea  you know what mike it was strategic and  it's not unheard of there are defense  attorneys who do that what they want to  do is to hear the prosecution's case and  they only want to address the necessary  issues any issues that the prosecution  might have raised but i think i would  have done an opening because the jury  wants to hear a story no matter what  that story is and of course the defense  attorney can always just say wait until  you hear all of the evidence some of the  evidence he could have talked about was  the evidence regarding the defendant's  mental health  regarding his background his family he  had a very difficult upbringing he did  not know his birth parents he could have  mentioned that during the opening or she  could have mentioned it the  prosecution's team could have mentioned  it and none of that was mentioned and it  might have helped because when the jury  is listening to all of the negative  horrible information in the back of  their minds they might be a little bit  more sensitive if they can hear that  there's mental illness involved terry  krista thank you for that analysis and  stay with us because still ahead on law  and crime daily we will continue our  coverage of the parkland school shooter  penalty phase trial please stay with us  [Music]  welcome back testimony in the parkland  school penalty phase trial began on july  18th as survivors and family members of  victims were called to the stand  alexander duret was both as he was shot  by the gunman and survived but his  brother was shot and killed  while i was sitting there you know just  trying not to  think this is real thinking it's you  know fake  um you know and then  just not trying to process you know  what's going on and then  um  while i'm sitting there 